module gunbc.build_cache_ensure


data build_cache_ensure_scope_disposition: std.disposition.Disposition = SingleAuthority

data ensure_effect_grain_migration_note: String = "WHY THE EFFECT GRAIN WAS WRONG, AND WHAT THIS PR DID AND DID NOT FIX. gunbc.host_effect's now-deleted ProvisionBuildCache carried a CacheInterfaceId, which names sccache's cache SEMANTICS and its pinned binary. It did not name a running daemon, and srv1 and srv2 each run TWO servers from that one catalog row — a CI server as ghrunner over /var/lib/ctrl/sccache-ci, and a session server as briansrls over /var/lib/ctrl/sccache with a 50G cap. So `provision the sccache_local catalog on this host` named NEITHER of them: it could not say which of the two to converge, and it could not express that one is correctly placed while the other is not. That was gunbc.build_cache_instance's grain note restated as an effect-layer defect.\n\nTHE SWAP LANDED (operator-cleared A5 un-defer, bounded to precisely the swap the census row specifies and nothing wider): HostEffect's constructible build-cache arm is now EnsureBuildCacheInstance { desired: BuildCacheInstance }, so the desired unit, principal, storage, capacity, endpoint and compile pool travel WITH the request instead of being guessed by a realizer. gunbc.host_build_cache_provision_script — the six hand-rolled *_body builders that used to answer the old arm — is DELETED; its shell-body construction (observe-stats, classify-failure, ensure-daemon, read-back) relocated into gunbc.host_effect_realize beside realize_provision_build_cache, which now matches on EnsureBuildCacheInstance and reads instance.implementation as the catalog gate key. Every consumer of the old arm (host_toolchain_ensure, host_build_cache_provision's model-only constructors, both design-witness and wet-execution test modules) was moved to the new shape in the same PR; no ProvisionBuildCache constructor remains reachable anywhere in the tree.\n\nPROVENANCE, TRACEABLE RATHER THAN ASSERTED: this note's own text is not the clearance, it is a record of one that exists elsewhere, because a clearance lifted by relay and never pointed at is indistinguishable from self-authorized dissolution (DESIGN's standing rule that a dissolution trigger is a lifecycle fact, not permission). The parent session (eager-crane-282) relayed the operator's instruction to open deferred srv* clusters at eager-crane-282's discretion of order, and dispatched build-cache first on the grounds it carries a live measured defect rather than only debt -- the un-defer of census row A5 for this cluster specifically. That same parent session then bounded the swap TWICE: an initial ruling to take the full arm-plus-render swap rather than a typed-argv-in-place relocation (rejecting relocation-counted-as-progress), followed by a narrower ruling that the swap's obligation is discharged by the arm and the render alone -- explicitly refusing to let the swap manufacture scope growth by wiring a consumer (admit_ensure_build_cache_instance) that does not yet exist in production. This PR's boundary -- arm renamed, bodies relocated behind render_build_cache_unit, admission left proven-but-unwired -- is that second, narrower ruling followed literally, not this PR's own inference from the first. The full exchange is on proud-swift-703's message thread with eager-crane-282, 2026-08-19 21:48 through 22:44 UTC, for a reviewer who wants the primary record rather than this summary.\n\nWHAT DID NOT LAND, STATED PLAINLY: RENDER IS NOT ADMIT. This PR's realize path still builds its shell script directly from the BuildCacheInstance travelling on the effect — via render_build_cache_unit for the unit body and the relocated *_body functions for the surrounding observe/classify/read-back shell — the same way the deleted script did. It does NOT construct or consume an EnsureBuildCacheAdmission from admit_ensure_build_cache_instance below: that function still takes a BuildCacheServerPlacement, an EndpointPathState and an ObservationCurrency that nothing in the realize path observes or threads today. Wiring the realize path through this module's admission — so the placement/path preflight this module already proves gates the daemon-ensure shell rather than being reasoned about only in tests — is real remaining work, not implied by this PR's title, and is explicitly out of this PR's scope per the same operator ruling that bounded the arm swap. It does not reach gunbc.host_build_cache_provision_script's own #5828 typed-argv dissolve-on trigger either: the shell body construction is relocated, not converted to typed ops.\n\nTHE NEXT CONSUMER NAMED ABOVE IS NOW THE ACTUAL CONSUMER (2026-08-21), and this paragraph is rewritten rather than annotated because a stale present-tense 'no production caller' in the carrier that exists to record reach is exactly the false claim it exists to prevent. realize_provision_build_cache_body in gunbc.host_effect_realize observes the target host through gunbc.build_cache_endpoint_observe -- the producer that did not exist when the paragraph above was written -- and routes through admit_ensure_build_cache_instance BEFORE it writes anything. EnsureAlreadyConverged returns converged with no unit written and no restart issued; EnsureBuildCacheRefused returns NotConverged carrying ensure_refusal_detail's located reason; only EnsureBuildCacheAdmitted reaches the daemon-ensure shell, and an EndpointClearedByUnlink preflight runs the admitted unlink as its own command first. What did NOT change is the shell body itself: the ensure step is still the relocated concat-built script, so #5828's typed-argv dissolve-on is untouched and this wiring is not it. All four clauses of the stale-socket observer contract ARE established by that producer, so EndpointStaleSocket is produced and the EndpointClearedByUnlink arm is reachable rather than dead. An earlier revision of this paragraph said the opposite -- that one clause needed a glob over /proc and a stale socket could therefore only ever refuse. That was a false capability claim, refuted by executing the walk as a single find argv, and it is corrected here rather than deleted because it had been written into several carriers as a reason not to try.\n\nAn earlier revision of this note said the replacement had not yet happened and pointed at the operator as its un-defer trigger. That trigger fired; the swap is in this tree; describing it as still deferred would now be the false present-tense claim this carrier exists to prevent."

type EndpointPreflight
  = EndpointClearForBind
  | EndpointClearedByUnlink { stale_detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

type EnsureRefusal
  = EnsureEndpointOccupied { owner: gunbc.build_cache_instance.ProcessIdentity }
  | EnsureEndpointPathConflict { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | EnsureEndpointUnobservable { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | EnsureUnlinkRefused { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | EnsurePlacementUnknown { detail: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

data ensure_is_a_reconcile_not_an_install_note: String = "ENSURE MUST BE IDEMPOTENT, WHICH IS WHY EnsureAlreadyConverged IS ITS OWN ARM RATHER THAN A SUCCESSFUL RE-INSTALL. The membership-diff spine's reading applies unchanged here: desired versus observed, and Unchanged is the ABSENCE of a hunk, not a cheap version of Added. If a correctly-placed managed unit already owns the endpoint, writing the unit again and restarting it would tear down a healthy cache and re-cold every compile on the host to reach a state it was already in — the most expensive possible no-op, and one that would fire on every convergence pass.\n\nDeciding it needs the OWNER, not the path. A listening endpoint tells you something holds it; only placement tells you whether that something is the unit we intend. So this admission takes both, and the placement authority is consulted FIRST: already-converged short-circuits before any path reasoning, because when the answer is 'nothing to do' the path state is irrelevant and reading it could only produce a spurious refusal."

type EnsureBuildCacheAdmission
  = EnsureAlreadyConverged { desired: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance, unit: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | EnsureBuildCacheAdmitted {
      desired: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance
      preflight: EndpointPreflight
      unit_lines: List<String>
    }
  | EnsureBuildCacheRefused { instance: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance, reason: EnsureRefusal }

data canonical_fold_note: String = "THE ELIMINATION SURFACE IS A FOLD, NOT A SCATTER OF MATCHES (review 46784, raised three times before it was taken). The predicates below previously each matched EnsureBuildCacheAdmission directly, which is legal — DESIGN section 4 names Match as one of the six closed behaviours — and that legality was the whole of my argument for keeping them. It was the wrong question. The right one is whether a coproduct with several consumers should have ONE named elimination surface, and it should: a fold record makes adding an arm a COMPILE ERROR at every consumer, because each literal must gain a field, whereas a scattered match silently keeps compiling and silently stops covering.\n\nThat is not theoretical here. Earlier in this same lane a fourth ProbeEffectOnSubjectFold literal in gunbc.claim_evidence_probe_rule was missed when an arm was added, and it was the FOLD RECORD that caught it — the compiler refused the incomplete literal. A hand-written match in that position would have gone on compiling against the old arm set. So the fold is the better construction for exactly the reason DESIGN prefers construction to validation, and the precedent to follow was already in the tree.\n\nBuildCacheServerPlacement is deliberately NOT folded here. It lives in gunbc.build_cache_instance with consumers already on main, so giving it a canonical fold is a broader refactor of a merged module rather than part of this change; placement_already_converged still matches it directly and is named as the residue that follow-up closes."

type EndpointPreflightFold<R> {
  clear_for_bind: R
  cleared_by_unlink: fn(std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> R
}

fn endpoint_preflight_fold<R>(preflight: EndpointPreflight, fold: EndpointPreflightFold<R>) -> R {
  match preflight {
    EndpointClearForBind => fold.clear_for_bind
    EndpointClearedByUnlink { stale_detail } => fold.cleared_by_unlink(stale_detail)
  }
}

type EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold<R> {
  already_converged: fn(BuildCacheInstance, std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> R
  admitted: fn(BuildCacheInstance, EndpointPreflight, List<String>) -> R
  refused: fn(BuildCacheInstance, EnsureRefusal) -> R
}

fn ensure_build_cache_admission_fold<R>(
  admission: EnsureBuildCacheAdmission,
  fold: EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold<R>,
) -> R {
  match admission {
    EnsureAlreadyConverged { desired, unit } => fold.already_converged(desired, unit)
    EnsureBuildCacheAdmitted { desired, preflight, unit_lines } =>
      fold.admitted(desired, preflight, unit_lines)
    EnsureBuildCacheRefused { instance, reason } => fold.refused(instance, reason)
  }
}

fn placement_already_converged(
  instance: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance,
  placement: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheServerPlacement,
) -> Bool {
  match placement {
    ServerManagedUnitOwned { unit: u } => (u as String) == (instance.intended_unit as String)
    ServerRunnerOwned { slot: _, lifecycle: _ } => false
    ServerUnsupervised { cgroup: _ } => false
    ServerPlacementAbsent => false
    ServerPlacementUnknown { detail: _ } => false
  }
}

fn admit_ensure_build_cache_instance(
  desired: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance,
  placement: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheServerPlacement,
  observed_path: gunbc.build_cache_endpoint_path.EndpointPathState,
  currency: gunbc.build_cache_endpoint_path.ObservationCurrency,
) -> EnsureBuildCacheAdmission {
  match placement_already_converged(instance: desired, placement: placement) {
    true => EnsureAlreadyConverged { desired: desired, unit: desired.intended_unit }
    false =>
      match placement {
        ServerPlacementUnknown { detail: d } =>
          EnsureBuildCacheRefused {
            instance: desired,
            reason: EnsurePlacementUnknown { detail: d },
          }
        ServerManagedUnitOwned { unit: _ } => ensure_from_path(desired: desired, observed_path: observed_path, currency: currency)
        ServerRunnerOwned { slot: _, lifecycle: _ } => ensure_from_path(desired: desired, observed_path: observed_path, currency: currency)
        ServerUnsupervised { cgroup: _ } => ensure_from_path(desired: desired, observed_path: observed_path, currency: currency)
        ServerPlacementAbsent => ensure_from_path(desired: desired, observed_path: observed_path, currency: currency)
      }
  }
}

data placement_unknown_refuses_before_the_path_note: String = "ServerPlacementUnknown REFUSES RATHER THAN FALLING THROUGH TO THE PATH. The other four non-converged placements are all POSITIVE readings — a runner owns it, something unsupervised owns it, nothing owns it, or a managed unit that is not ours owns it — and in each of those the path state is the right next question, because we know we must act and only need to know whether the endpoint is clear. Unknown is not a reading at all: the observer could not classify the owner's cgroup. Proceeding on it would mean deciding to write and restart a unit on a host whose current state we failed to read, which is the absorbing fallback in its most expensive form — the widen here is not a rerun, it is a host mutation.\n\nThe distinction is worth the extra arm precisely because Unknown and Absent are easy to conflate: both mean 'we found no managed owner', but Absent means we LOOKED and there is none, while Unknown means the looking failed."

fn ensure_from_path(
  desired: gunbc.build_cache_instance.BuildCacheInstance,
  observed_path: gunbc.build_cache_endpoint_path.EndpointPathState,
  currency: gunbc.build_cache_endpoint_path.ObservationCurrency,
) -> EnsureBuildCacheAdmission {
  match observed_path {
    EndpointPathAbsent =>
      EnsureBuildCacheAdmitted {
        desired: desired,
        preflight: EndpointClearForBind,
        unit_lines: render_build_cache_unit(instance: desired),
      }
    EndpointListening { owner: o } =>
      EnsureBuildCacheRefused { instance: desired, reason: EnsureEndpointOccupied { owner: o } }
    EndpointPathConflict { detail: d } =>
      EnsureBuildCacheRefused { instance: desired, reason: EnsureEndpointPathConflict { detail: d } }
    EndpointPathObservationUnavailable { reason: _ } =>
      EnsureBuildCacheRefused {
        instance: desired,
        reason: EnsureEndpointUnobservable {
          detail: "the endpoint path could not be read, so whether a bind would succeed is unknown; refusing rather than writing and starting a unit into an unread state" as std.types.NonEmptyStr,
        },
      }
    EndpointStaleSocket { detail: _ } =>
      match admit_stale_socket_unlink(instance: desired, observed: observed_path, currency: currency) {
        StaleSocketUnlinkRefused { instance: _, reason: r } =>
          EnsureBuildCacheRefused {
            instance: desired,
            reason: EnsureUnlinkRefused { detail: unlink_refusal_detail(reason: r) },
          }
        StaleSocketUnlinkAdmitted { instance: _, stale_detail: sd, observed_at_boot: _ } =>
          EnsureBuildCacheAdmitted {
            desired: desired,
            preflight: EndpointClearedByUnlink { stale_detail: sd },
            unit_lines: render_build_cache_unit(instance: desired),
          }
      }
  }
}

data occupied_endpoint_refuses_note: String = "AN OCCUPIED ENDPOINT REFUSES EVEN THOUGH WE KNOW THE OWNER IS WRONG, and that is deliberate rather than timid. Reaching this arm means placement already said the owner is not our intended unit — a runner-owned server, an unsupervised one, or another unit's — so it is tempting to treat 'wrong owner holding the socket' as licence to take it. It is not, for the same reason the unlink admission refuses a listener: the holder is a LIVE process with in-flight compiles as its children, and severing or replacing its socket strands those compiles and leaves a server running that nothing can reach.\n\nEvicting a live wrong owner is a genuinely different operation from ensuring a unit exists — it requires draining the runners whose compiles are that server's children, and it can fail halfway. Folding it into ensure would make an idempotent converge step conditionally destructive. It gets its own admission when the quiesce path is modeled; until then this refusal is the honest boundary and it names the owner so the operator can see exactly which process is in the way."

fn ensure_yields_unit(admission: EnsureBuildCacheAdmission) -> Bool {
  ensure_build_cache_admission_fold(
    admission: admission,
    fold: EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold {
      already_converged: fn(_, _) { false },
      admitted: fn(_, _, _) { true },
      refused: fn(_, _) { false },
    },
  )
}

// Named for its admission type, not generically, and the generic spelling is what broke main. This was
// `ensure_is_converged`; gunbc.compile_pool_ensure declared the same name over EnsureCompilePoolAdmission
// (#7937), and both witness modules referenced it BARE, so the containment tree had two candidates for one
// reference and refused all seven call sites — including the four here, in a module that PR did not touch.
// Neither helper is generic across the two admission types, so the shared spelling was never carrying a
// shared concept; it was a homonym (DESIGN §3), and the rename is the repair.
//
// WHY THE RENAME STILL LANDED AFTER main WAS ALREADY UNBLOCKED. #7984 restored main by adding explicit
// import lists to the two witness modules, which supplies the missing authority at each reference and is a
// correct unblock. It leaves the homonym standing. Under an import the ambiguity is resolved; under the
// next BARE reference from anywhere in the tree it returns unchanged, and per-PR selection will hide that
// one exactly as it hid this one — a reduced pool reaches one definition, the author sees green, and the
// whole-tree pool reds after merge. Disambiguating a reference treats the occurrence; renaming the
// declarations removes the class. The two compose rather than compete: the imports remain and now name
// symbols that cannot collide.
//
// WHAT MADE IT INVISIBLE, which is the larger defect and is NOT fixed here. #7937 was green because per-PR
// affected-set selection loads a reduced declaration pool in which only one definition was reachable; the
// whole-tree pool loads both and the same source stops resolving. So reference binding changed as a
// function of which UNRELATED files CI chose to load. Selection is supposed to alter cost and execution
// scope, never name-resolution semantics — the property owed is that
// resolve(reference, closure) == resolve(reference, closure + unrelated modules), with a bare reference
// lacking structural authority refusing under BOTH pools rather than passing under the smaller one.
// Its discriminating control compiles one test module twice, against the intended pool and against that
// pool plus a module carrying a same-spelled declaration, and requires either identical binding identity
// or the same missing-authority refusal from both. That control belongs to the namespace / reference-derived
// closure lane and is deliberately not here (operator direction, 2026-08-07): restore main first, do not
// delay it for a resolver redesign. Note that neither this rename nor #7984's imports discharge it — both
// remove instances, and the property is about the resolver.
fn build_cache_ensure_is_converged(admission: EnsureBuildCacheAdmission) -> Bool {
  ensure_build_cache_admission_fold(
    admission: admission,
    fold: EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold {
      already_converged: fn(_, _) { true },
      admitted: fn(_, _, _) { false },
      refused: fn(_, _) { false },
    },
  )
}

fn preflight_needed_an_unlink(preflight: EndpointPreflight) -> Bool {
  endpoint_preflight_fold(
    preflight: preflight,
    fold: EndpointPreflightFold {
      clear_for_bind: false,
      cleared_by_unlink: fn(_) { true },
    },
  )
}

fn ensure_requires_unlink_first(admission: EnsureBuildCacheAdmission) -> Bool {
  ensure_build_cache_admission_fold(
    admission: admission,
    fold: EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold {
      already_converged: fn(_, _) { false },
      admitted: fn(_, p, _) { preflight_needed_an_unlink(preflight: p) },
      refused: fn(_, _) { false },
    },
  )
}

data refusal_text_is_a_diagnostic_not_the_contract_note: String = "THE REFUSAL'S TEXT IS RENDERED HERE, BESIDE THE COPRODUCT IT RENDERS, so a consumer at a realization boundary reports the refusal without re-deciding what it means. gunbc.host_build_cache_provision provision_gate_consumes_the_fold_note already fixed the rule this follows: what a verdict CONTRACTUALLY says is its arm and its cause, and the located prose is a diagnostic payload. A realizer writing its own sentence per arm would be a second rendering of one fact that typechecks while it drifts, and would silently stop covering the day an arm is added -- so the rendering lives with the type and every consumer quotes it."

fn ensure_refusal_detail(reason: EnsureRefusal) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match reason {
    EnsureEndpointOccupied { owner: o } =>
      concat(
        concat("a live process holds the endpoint and it is not the intended managed unit; pid ", to_string(o.pid)),
        concat(
          concat(" under boot ", o.boot_id as String),
          ". Evicting a live owner strands the compiles that are its children, so ensure refuses rather than taking the socket; draining is a separate operation.",
        ),
      ) as NonEmptyStr
    EnsureEndpointPathConflict { detail: d } => d
    EnsureEndpointUnobservable { detail: d } => d
    EnsureUnlinkRefused { detail: d } => d
    EnsurePlacementUnknown { detail: d } =>
      concat(
        "the endpoint owner's placement could not be observed, and writing plus restarting a unit on a host whose current state we failed to read is a host mutation decided on ignorance: ",
        d as String,
      ) as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn ensure_admission_refusal_reason(admission: EnsureBuildCacheAdmission) -> String {
  ensure_build_cache_admission_fold(
    admission: admission,
    fold: EnsureBuildCacheAdmissionFold {
      already_converged: fn(_, u) {
        concat("build_cache_ensure: already converged on unit ", u as String)
      },
      admitted: fn(_, _, _) {
        "build_cache_ensure: admitted"
      },
      refused: fn(i, r) {
        concat(
          concat("build_cache_ensure: refused ensuring ", i.intended_unit as String),
          concat(" -- ", ensure_refusal_detail(reason: r) as String),
        )
      },
    },
  )
}
